+# Why production RPC traffic lands on `mainnet.base.org`
+
+## The exact chain
+
+**1. `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` is a build-time constant, not a runtime read.**
+In Next.js, `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_*` references are not read from the
+environment at runtime. During `next build`, webpack's DefinePlugin does a
+static *textual* substitution: every occurrence of
+`process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` in the code is replaced with the literal
+string value that was present **in the build environment** at the moment
+`next build` ran. The value comes from the process env plus any
+`.env` / `.env.local` / `.env.production` files present in the repo at build
+time. If the var is unset at build time, the reference is inlined as the
+literal `undefined`.
+
+**2. The value that gets baked in differs between dev and prod.**
+- Developer machines have `.env.local` (gitignored, so it is *not* in the
+  repo or CI) containing `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=<paid RPC>`. `next dev`/`next
+  build` inlines the paid URL → all dev traffic uses the paid RPC → no 429s.
+- The production build (CI / hosting build step) runs without that file and,
+  evidently, without the var set in its build env. So
+  `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` inlines to `undefined`.
+
+**3. This is a static export on generic hosting — there is no runtime to fix it.**
+A static Next.js build has no Node server, so there is no runtime env
+injection. Whatever was baked at build time is frozen into the JS bundle the
+host serves. Setting the var in the host's runtime env panel does nothing.
+
+**4. So `rpcOverrides[base.id]` is `undefined` in the shipped bundle.**
+```ts
+rpcOverrides: { [chains.base.id]: undefined }   // as compiled into prod
+```
+
+**5. `wagmiConfig.tsx` then falls through to a bare `http()`.**
+In `client({ chain })`:
+```ts
+let rpcFallbacks = [http()];
+const rpcOverrideUrl = scaffoldConfig.rpcOverrides?.[chain.id]; // undefined
+if (rpcOverrideUrl) { ... }        // skipped
+else { /* alchemy override, none configured for Base → stays [http()] */ }
+transport: fallback(rpcFallbacks)  // fallback([http()])
+```
+`http()` called with **no URL argument** makes viem use the chain's default
+public endpoint, `chain.rpcUrls.default` — for Base that is
+`https://mainnet.base.org`.
+
+**6. Every production visitor hammers the public endpoint → 429.**
+Dev looked fine only because dev had the paid URL inlined; nobody noticed the
+prod bundle was built without it.
+
+## The one operational check that settles whether the fix is real
+
+**Inspect the deployed artifact, not the host's env settings.** Because the
+value is inlined at build time, the built bundle is the source of truth. After
+adding `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` to the **build** environment and **rebuilding +
+redeploying**, verify against the live site:
+
+```bash
+# grep the deployed static JS for the hosts
+curl -s https://<your-site>/_next/static/chunks/*.js | grep -o 'mainnet\.base\.org'      # should be gone as primary
+curl -s https://<your-site>/_next/static/chunks/*.js | grep -o '<paid-rpc-host>'         # should appear
+```
+Equivalently: open the live site, watch the browser Network tab, and confirm
+the `eth_*` JSON-RPC POSTs go to the paid host, not `mainnet.base.org`.
+
+The point of the check: a fix applied in the host's *runtime* env is a no-op;
+only the presence of the paid host **inside the shipped bundle** proves the
+rebuild picked up the var. If the bundle still contains `mainnet.base.org` as
+the request target, the fix did not take.
+
+## The second, transport-level trap (survives fixing the env var)
+
+Even with the override correctly set, the transport is built as a **fallback
+with the bare public endpoint still wired in as the backup**:
+
+```ts
+if (rpcOverrideUrl) {
+  rpcFallbacks = [http(rpcOverrideUrl), http()];   // <-- second http() = public mainnet.base.org
+}
+...
+transport: fallback(rpcFallbacks)
+```
+
+viem's `fallback` transport automatically fails over to the **next** transport
+whenever the current one errors — and that includes your paid RPC returning
+429/5xx, timing out, or hiccuping under load. So the moment the paid endpoint
+is rate-limited or slow, requests silently spill onto `http()` →
+`mainnet.base.org`, producing exactly the 429s ops is seeing — now from the
+*fallback* path rather than the missing-override path. (If `rank` were enabled,
+viem would also periodically ping every endpoint, sending baseline traffic to
+the public RPC regardless of health.)
+
+**Fix:** for chains where you have a paid RPC, don't leave a bare public
+`http()` as the fallback — either drop it (`fallback([http(rpcOverrideUrl)])`)
+or make the fallback a second authenticated/paid endpoint. Otherwise the public
+RPC is never actually removed from the request path; it's just demoted.
+
+## Summary
+- Root cause: `NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL` inlined as `undefined` because the prod
+  **build** ran without it; static export has no runtime to inject it later.
+  Missing override → bare `http()` → Base public RPC.
+- Definitive check: the paid host must appear in the **deployed bundle** /
+  live Network requests after a rebuild — runtime env changes don't count.
+- Second trap: `fallback([http(override), http()])` keeps the public RPC as an
+  auto-failover, so paid-RPC errors/timeouts still leak requests to
+  `mainnet.base.org`.

